{"response":"Looking at the actual code, `GeminiClient.java` is almost entirely platform-agnostic — it's just OkHttp + Jackson + string processing. The only platform coupling is the **API key**, which flows through `AppConfig`. Here's how to handle it in a shared-module architecture:

---

### The pattern: Interface in shared core, implementation in each platform

**In `bahai-search-core`:**

```java
// Shared interface — no platform dependency
public interface AiService {
    ResearchReport generateReport(String topic);
    LocalQueryIntent resolveLocalQueryIntent(String topic, List<String> knownWorkTitles);
    List<Integer> rerankLocalCandidates(String topic, List<CorpusSearchHit> candidates, int maxQuotes);
}
```

```java
// ResearchService in shared core — depends on the interface, not GeminiClient
public class ResearchService {
    private final AiService aiService;  // injected

    public ResearchReport conductResearch(String topic) {
        // ... local FTS5 first ...
        if (localHits exist) return localResults;
        if (aiService == null) return noResults;  // Android: no AI, just bail
        return aiService.generateReport(topic);   // Windows: call Gemini
    }
}
```

**In `bahai-windows`:**

```java
// Windows implementation — GeminiClient stays exactly as-is, just implements the interface
public class WindowsAiService implements AiService {
    private final GeminiClient geminiClient;

    public WindowsAiService(AppConfig config) {
        this.geminiClient = new GeminiClient(config);  // key from env var → config file
    }

    @Override
    public ResearchReport generateReport(String topic) {
        return geminiClient.generateReport(topic, appConfig);
    }
    // ... delegate other methods ...
}
```

**In `bahai-android`:**

```java
// Android implementation — no AI at all, or a stub
public class AndroidAiService implements AiService {
    @Override
    public ResearchReport generateReport(String topic) {
        return new ResearchReport("AI not available on mobile", List.of());
    }

    @Override
    public LocalQueryIntent resolveLocalQueryIntent(String topic, List<String> knownWorkTitles) {
        return new LocalQueryIntent("", "", "", List.of());
    }
    // ... etc ...
}
```

---

### Alternative: Optional dependency (simpler, what you'd actually do)

Since the Android app already has no Gemini dependency at all, you don't even need the interface pattern. Just:

1. **Move `GeminiClient.java` into the shared core** — it compiles everywhere (pure Java + OkHttp + Jackson)
2. **Add it as a `compileOnly` dependency on Android** (the class exists at compile time, but Android never instantiates it because `geminiApiKey` is blank)
3. **Keep the existing guard clause**: `if (appConfig.geminiApiKey().isBlank()) return fallback;`

This works because GeminiClient's own code already handles the "no key" case gracefully — it checks `geminiApiKey().isBlank()` in `generateReport()`, `resolveLocalQueryIntent()`, and `rerankLocalCandidates()`. The class can *exist* in the Android build — it just never makes network calls because the API key is empty string on that platform.

---

### The actual answer to your question

**GeminiClient doesn't need special handling in a shared module.** It has zero platform-specific code. The only difference between Windows and Android is:

| | Windows `AppConfig` | Android `AppConfig` |
|---|---|---|
| `geminiApiKey` | Loaded from env var → key file | `""` (empty string) |
| `geminiModel` | `"gemini-2.5-flash"` | `""` |
| Behavior | Full Gemini calls | Every method returns empty/fallback |

The class itself stays the same. The `AppConfig` object is what's different per platform. So the answer is: **`GeminiClient` would live in the shared core untouched, and each platform provides its own `AppConfig`** (Windows reads env vars, Android uses empty strings). The existing guard clauses already handle both paths correctly.","options":[]}

