{"response":"Android doesn't run on a standard JVM — it has its own runtime (ART, formerly Dalvik). So even though you compile with Java 11 in your `build.gradle`, the Android device doesn't actually have a Java 11 runtime. It has whatever the device's API level provides.

### The Record Problem

Java `record` was introduced in Java 14 (preview) / Java 16 (final). On Android, `record` isn't available natively until **API level 34** (Android 14), because that's when Google added `java.lang.Record` to the Android runtime.

Your project targets **`minSdk 24`** (Android 7.0 Nougat, 2016). That means:
- If you use `record` in your code, it will **crash** on any device below Android 14
- The overwhelming majority of users in 2026 are still on Android 13 or below

### The Fix: Core Library Desugaring

Google provides a tool called **"desugaring"** that backports newer Java APIs to older devices. You add this to `build.gradle`:

```groovy
android {
    compileOptions {
        coreLibraryDesugaringEnabled true   // ← enable it
        sourceCompatibility JavaVersion.VERSION_11
        targetCompatibility JavaVersion.VERSION_11
    }
}

dependencies {
    coreLibraryDesugaring 'com.android.tools:desugar_jdk_libs:2.1.5'
}
```

This would allow `record` to work all the way back to `minSdk 24`. Many apps do this — it's well-supported and adds ~2MB to the APK.

### The Simpler Alternative: Stay with Plain Classes

Your Android code currently uses plain Java classes for `CorpusSearchHit`, `QuoteResult`, and `ResearchReport` — no records. This works everywhere, forever, with zero configuration. They look like:

```java
// Plain class — 100% compatible with all Android versions
public final class CorpusSearchHit {
    private final String quote;
    private final String author;
    private final String title;
    private final String locator;
    private final String sourceUrl;
    private final double score;

    public CorpusSearchHit(String quote, ...) { ... }
    public String quote() { return quote; }
    // ...
}
```

vs. the Windows version which uses:

```java
// Record — compact, but needs Java 16+ or desugaring
public record CorpusSearchHit(
    String quote,
    String author,
    String title,
    String locator,
    String sourceUrl,
    double score
) {}
```

### My Recommendation

Use **plain classes** in the shared `.jar`. Here's why:

| | Records | Plain Classes |
|---|---|---|
| Android compatibility | Needs desugaring or minSdk 34 | Works on all API levels |
| Windows compatibility | ✅ Native | ✅ Native |
| Maintenance burden | Less code | Slightly more code |
| Risk | Introduces new build dependency | Zero risk |
| Functional difference | None | None |

The shared models are 3 small classes (~15-30 lines each). The verbosity difference is negligible. Meanwhile, `SearchCore` (the real prize — ~500 lines of algorithm logic) doesn't use records at all — it's all static methods with collections and strings.

The Windows project can consume plain classes just fine. Java 21 doesn't care whether an object was defined as a `record` or a `class` — it's all bytecode at runtime.

Want me to proceed with the plan on this basis (plain classes, no desugaring)?","options":[]}

